+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md for motivation. Current-state facts the approach rests on (all verified in this
+checkout):
+
+- `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/session_hook.py::_end_impl` already holds the validated host session
+  id in `session` (from `_session(payload)`: regex-bounded to at most 128 visible-ASCII
+  characters, cross-checked against `GRAPHHELM_SESSION_ID`). The end `POST .../signal` call is
+  the single request site in `_end_impl` and passes its headers as one inline dict literal
+  (`X-GraphHelm-Actor`, `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Type`, `Idempotency-Key`).
+- The Runtime signal endpoint already reads, bounds, and persists
+  `x-graphhelm-actor-session` as an optional presence declaration
+  (`apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs`, `declared_session`, issue #1057). No Rust, schema, or
+  wire-schema change is needed for this header to take effect.
+- The hook test suite runs the script as a subprocess against an in-process `http.server`
+  (`RuntimeHandler.requests` captures `(method, path, dict(self.headers), body)`). The existing
+  end-request test asserts captured header keys with per-segment Title Case, e.g.
+  `sent[0][2]["X-Graphhelm-Actor-Type"]` — a casing produced by `http.server`'s header parsing
+  on Python 3.11, verified empirically on this machine (wire bytes are `urllib`-capitalized,
+  e.g. `x-graphhelm-actor-session` form, and are normalized back to Title Case on read).
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- The end request declares the host session id via `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session`.
+- The one existing end-request contract test asserts the header; the rest of the suite stays
+  green unmodified.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- No Runtime/Rust changes, no schema changes, no new dependencies.
+- No change to the start (briefing) request, to `request()` itself, or to retry/idempotency
+  semantics. No new header helper function.
+
+## Decisions
+
+1. **Header value is the existing `session` variable, taken as-is.** It is validated upstream
+   (regex, length, environment binding) before any HTTP happens. *Alternative rejected:* parsing
+   the session out of the composite actor id `<host>-session-<id>` — unnecessary string surgery
+   on a value that can itself contain dashes or dots, when the validated original is already in
+   scope.
+2. **Add the key inline to the existing headers dict literal** at the end-request call site
+   (`"X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session": session`). *Alternatives rejected:* a new helper function
+   (explicitly out of scope for this request); folding it into `request()`'s defaults (would
+   put an end-only concern on the shared path used by the start request too).
+3. **Retries recompute the header from the payload, which is safe.** Delivery receipts are
+   scoped by session: the state identity check in `_read_state` refuses to load a receipt whose
+   `sessionId` differs before any POST, so every request made under one receipt carries the same
+   value. No receipt-format change, no stored field added.
+4. **Test assertion uses the normalized key `sent[0][2]["X-Graphhelm-Actor-Session"] ==
+   "session-123"`**, matching the established style of the adjacent
+   `X-Graphhelm-Actor-Type` assertion. *Alternative rejected:* making the handler normalize keys
+   case-insensitively — larger surface than the request allows, and the current style is what the
+   green suite already relies on.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- **Observed header casing depends on `http.server`'s parser normalization** (Python 3.11
+  behavior, verified here). A future swap of the test's HTTP layer could shift the key spelling
+  → the assertion sits next to its sibling assertion; both would need updating together, which
+  makes the shift visible rather than silent.
+- **Presence-event counts are unchanged by design:** the Runtime appends an
+  `agent_presence_declared` event only when a declaration differs from the actor's newest one
+  (#1054). Repeated identical ends still append the signal only — now with a session attached.
+  No mitigation needed; noted so reviewers do not mistake flat event counts for a miss.
+- **Older Runtimes ignore the new header** (optional, additive) → mixed-version operation is
+  safe in either order; nothing to migrate.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+One small change touching the two named files. Rollback is reverting those files: the header
+disappears from the wire and the Runtime records absence again, which it already handles.
